  "production_date": "about 1640",
  "brief_description": "This painting demonstrates Frans Hals's gift for creating lively and animated portraits that suggest distinctive personalities. We don't know who the woman in this compelling work is, but her elegant dress and jewellery indicate that, like many of Hals's patrons, she may have been the wife of a wealthy citizen of Haarlem.\n\nHals has paid great attention to the detail of her costume, which is characteristic of the period around 1640, when this picture was probably painted. She holds an expensive fan made of feathers, set in a gold mount and suspended from a gold chain. This object functions as a status symbol but also serves to give the woman's pose movement. She pulls playfully on the chain with one hand and holds the fan's mount delicately between the thumb and ring finger of the other -- a position that could change momentarily.",
